+On a Windows checkout with Git's `core.autocrlf=true` (the common Windows default), 2 of
+these 20 tests currently fail — `MapiMessageEmlTests.LoadFromEmlHandlesFoldedHeadersAndBase64Text`
+and `MapiMessageEmlTests.LoadFromEmlHandlesMultipartRelatedInlineAttachment`. Both build their
+synthetic EML fixture from a raw string literal and then call `.Replace("\n", "\r\n")` on it;
+when the source file itself already has CRLF line endings (what `autocrlf=true` produces), this
+doubles each line's carriage return and corrupts the fixture before the library ever parses it.
+This is a test-fixture issue, not a defect in the library's own EML/MIME parsing — a checkout with
+`core.autocrlf=false` or `input`, or LF-normalized sources, does not trigger it.
